    "body": "@TheBlek - I'm seeing some errors when raycasting against particularly small triangles:\r\n\r\n<img width=\"400\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/151d3899-23ee-4b89-9685-727c2d9ef13e\" />\r\n\r\nThis happens when setting the sphere geometry radius to a small value and compensating by increasing the ball scale:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nconst sphereGeom = new SphereGeometry( 0.1, 64, 32 );\r\n// ...\r\nball1.scale.setScalar( 5 );\r\nball2.scale.setScalar( 5 );\r\nball3.scale.setScalar( 5 );\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe issue is probably a combination of small edge lengths being used for cross products in the triangle intersection test as well as the ray direction length becoming small when transforming into the local coordinate frame of 5x scale transform matrix (ray direction will be transformed to 1/5th size). I'm wondering if theres a way to make the triangle intersection test more robust to this? Or some other approach to prevent these kinds of issues?\r\n\r\n_edit_\r\n\r\nIf we _don't_ scale the transforms up then things look okay, so this may primarily be an issue of the ray direction length?\r\n\r\n<img width=\"400\" alt=\"image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/82ef6ee3-a558-49a8-af1d-8e34f70000ef\" />",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
